the most profound discovery of our time  is that our universe had a beginning and  that it's expanding yet the person  responsible for this discovery is  virtually unknown and there's a reason  for it which will become clear je le  metro was a belgian priest who theorized  that the expansion of the universe began  with the explosion of a single particle  at a definite point in time faster than  the speed of light the big bang theory  is accepted by nearly all astronomers  today but back in the 1930s the belief  that the entire observable universe  began with a bang was considered  preposterous even albert einstein found  it far-fetched so how did lumet come up  with this theory  let's go back to the beginning  [Music]  belgium in 1894 to a well-off developing  catholic family he knew what he wanted  to be when he was nine years old both a  priest and a scientist he grew up in a  coal mining region and at the insistence  of his father studied civil mining  engineering at the catholic research  university in the belgian city of luva  only to have his studies interrupted  when germany invaded belgium in 1914. he  left university to fight as an artillery  officer and received the belgian war  cross for bravery on the battlefield at  this time he was already thinking about  the origin of the universe in the  context of his christian faith he wrote  to a friend while in the trenches of war  i've understood the fiat looks as the  reason of the universe fiat looks is  latin for let there be light in  reference to the third verse in the book  of genesis god said let there be light  and there was light after the war he  continued with his education but  switched to mathematics and physics  graduating in 1920 then he went to  seminary school and became ordained as a  priest three years later during this  time he became fascinated by einstein's  general theory of relativity how gravity  can warp space and time which i talked  about in a previous video lumetri then  did postgraduate work at the university  of cambridge under famed english  astronomer sir arthur eddington who had  confirmed einstein's theory when he saw  how gravity bent light from a star  traveling near the sun eddington gave  lumetri a glowing recommendation after  which he spent time at harvard and then  got accepted into a doctoral program at  mit it was a good time to be in the u.s  if you were a scientist because of  recent discoveries that shook people's  understanding of the universe up until  then the milky way which includes our  solar system was thought to be the  entire universe but more powerful  telescopes showed this wasn't true  american astronomer edwin hubble  announced he had evidence the milky way  was one of many galaxies in the universe  he observed that the spiral-shaped  andromeda was in fact a galaxy another  american astronomer vesto slifer noticed  andromeda was bluer in color whereas  other galaxies were redder slifer knew  andromeda was approaching us because of  light is coming toward you the waves get  more squished making the light bluer if  it goes away from you it gets stretched  out making the light waves appear redder  the light of distant galaxies that  appeared to shift more toward the red  end of the color spectrum suggested they  were getting farther away from earth the  belief was that the galaxies were moving  away in a static universe however  lumetra interpreted this red shift  differently he concluded that the  galaxies were not moving away from each  other in a static universe but rather  the universe itself was expanding which  then stretched out the light waves  here's another way to look at it imagine  that this balloon is the universe and  the dots the galaxies as the balloon  expands then the separation between the  dots also expands and from the  perspective of each individual dot or  galaxy it appears that the others are  moving farther away from it an expanding  universe completely shattered the notion  that the universe was static which is  what einstein strongly believed but an  unchanging universe was problematic for  his own theory of relativity if gravity  were the only active force in a static  universe the universe would collapse to  get around this issue einstein added a  cosmological constant presumably a form  of matter or energy that opposes gravity  on the other hand lumetra's expanding  universe would satisfy einstein's theory  of relativity still einstein wasn't  swayed he refused to believe in an  expanding universe lumetra recalled  einstein told him your calculations are  correct but your physics is atrocious  it would take einstein a long time to  come around he did finally abandon his  cosmological constant calling it the  greatest blunder of his life  lumetro returned to europe in 1925 to  take up a teaching post at the catholic  university of louvain where he was  formerly a student in 1927 he published  his case for an expanding universe in  french in a belgian scientific journal  this should have rocked the world but it  didn't no one really paid any attention  to it it wasn't until two years later  when hubble published his own findings  about an expanding universe that the  world took notice hubble measured the  distance of galaxies and also built on  the work of slifer who had measured  redshifts he plotted the distance of  galaxies in their velocities in this  classic graph and showed that more  distant galaxies were moving away from  us faster than galaxies that were closer  and it made sense if the universe were  expanding this became known as hubble's  law he came to the same conclusion as  lumetra yet lementra didn't get any of  the attention and accolades bestowed on  hubble because lumetra had published his  findings in french in an obscure belgian  journal that wasn't widely read outside  of his own country his mentor eddington  is said to have helped arrange an  english translation two years after the  release of hubble's work in 1931.  however the paper omitted limetra's  crucial paragraphs describing hubble's  law turns out lymetra chose to leave  this out himself he reasoned that  hubble's calculations had already  improved on his earlier work he  apparently wasn't fussed at proving that  he had come up with the expansion theory  before hubble he was a humble man who  didn't mind that hubble got the glory  instead lumetra moved forward with his  research and published a new paper in  1931 in which he argued that if the  universe were expanding then it also had  a beginning he proposed this in his  hypothesis of the primeval atom a  primeval atom was a single particle he  argued that the universe began with the  explosion of the single particle that  gave birth to our entire cosmos and  concluded a creation-like event had  occurred researchers date the event to  more than 13.8 billion years ago he  didn't call it the big bang astronomer  sir fred hoyle coined the term during a  talk broadcast on the bbc hoyle actually  thought the theory was nonsense many  scientists were skeptical including  einstein however in 1933 when lametre  and einstein traveled together to attend  a series of seminars in california  elemetra had time to explain his theory  in detail einstein is purported to have  said this is the most beautiful and  satisfactory explanation of creation to  which i have ever listened le metzkel  was nominated for the nobel prize in  physics in 1954 for his prediction of  the expanding universe two years later  he was nominated for the nobel prize in  chemistry for his primeval atom theory  he didn't win either the strongest  evidence supporting the big bang was  discovered by accident a decade later in  1964 by american scientists arno penzias  and robert wilson who were working for  bell laboratories the research and  development arm of what is today nokia  they were baffled by a strange signal  noise picked up by this antenna and  tried to figure out what it was they  even removed some pigeons that were  nesting there that's when they stumbled  upon cosmic microwave background  radiation ancient light thought to be  left over from the big bang lametre  lived to see the moment of their  discovery his ideas were finally  vindicated he died two years later in  1966 from leukemia at the age of 71.  despite not being as arguably well known  as his fellow scientists he is finally  getting more recognition in 2018 the  hubble law was changed to the hubble la  metro law  la metro may have helped uncover the  secrets of the universe yet the more we  learn the stranger it gets  scientists thought that the expansion of  the universe would gradually slow down  over time as gravity put on the brakes  but surprisingly they found the opposite  turns out the expansion of the universe  is accelerating some mysterious force is  driving our universe apart and we don't  know why perhaps in time this mystery  will also be solved this video is  sponsored by skillshare skillshare is  like the netflix of educational videos  it's an online learning community where  you get access to thousands of classes  on a variety of topics you can learn how  to edit videos how to speak confidently  on camera and if you like to  procrastinate there's a class on how to  be more productive there's literally  something for everyone each class is  broken up into short sections and there  are no tests they're great if you're a  beginner or if you'd like to brush up on  your skills skillshare is free for you  to try out for a month if you use my  custom link in the description thanks so  much for watching i hope you enjoyed it  if you did don't forget to subscribe and  give it a like for news think i'm cindy  pomp ah you're so cute this fella says  thanks as well  [Music]
 

